{"causes": ["The expansion of Polish political and ecclesiastical influence eastward into Red Ruthenia necessitated the establishment of a robust Latin Rite hierarchy.", "The formal establishment of the Latin Archdiocese of Halicz in 1375 created a new administrative structure requiring dedicated leadership.", "The presence of the Franciscan Order in Eastern Europe provided a pool of dedicated and learned individuals suitable for high ecclesiastical office."], "effects": ["Jakub Strzemię's episcopate significantly strengthened the institutional presence and organization of the Latin Rite Catholic Church in Red Ruthenia.", "His death and the subsequent incorporation of the Halicz archdiocese into Lviv facilitated the relocation of the primary Catholic see to a more strategically central city.", "His beatification in 1790 solidified his spiritual veneration, particularly within the Franciscan order and the Catholic community of historical Galicia."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "pope-boniface-ix", "sourceName": "Pope Boniface IX", "verb": "CANONIZES", "targetSlug": "jakub-strzemię", "targetName": "Jakub Strzemię", "context": "Pope Boniface IX appointed Jakub Strzemię as the Archbishop of Halicz in 1392, entrusting him with the spiritual leadership of the archdiocese."}, {"sourceSlug": "jakub-strzemię", "sourceName": "Jakub Strzemię", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "order-of-friars-minor", "targetName": "Order of Friars Minor", "context": "Jakub Strzemię was a professed member of the Franciscan Order, which shaped his spiritual formation and administrative approach."}, {"sourceSlug": "jakub-strzemię", "sourceName": "Jakub Strzemię", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "lviv", "targetName": "Lviv", "context": "Jakub Strzemię's archdiocese of Halicz was ultimately incorporated into the Archdiocese of Lviv after his death in 1409."}, {"sourceSlug": "jakub-strzemię", "sourceName": "Jakub Strzemię", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "latin-rite-catholicism-in-galicia", "targetName": "Latin Rite Catholicism in Galicia", "context": "Jakub Strzemię's archiepiscopate was instrumental in strengthening the institutional structures and presence of Latin Rite Catholicism in the historical region of Galicia."}, {"sourceSlug": "kingdom-of-poland-jagiellonian", "sourceName": "Kingdom of Poland (Jagiellonian)", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "jakub-strzemię", "targetName": "Jakub Strzemię", "context": "Jakub Strzemię's archdiocese of Halicz operated within the expanding territorial boundaries and political influence of the Jagiellonian Kingdom of Poland."}], "places": [{"name": "Halicz, Poland (Historical Galicia)", "role": "Archiepiscopal See"}, {"name": "Lviv, Ukraine", "role": "Successor See"}, {"name": "Kraków, Poland", "role": "Ecclesiastical Center"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Jakub Strzemię", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "jakub-strzemię"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q11716598", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jakub_Strzemi%C4%99"], "tags": ["Religious Figures", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Jakub Strzemię played a crucial role in consolidating the Latin Rite Catholic Church's presence and administrative structure in Red Ruthenia during a period of significant geopolitical and religious flux. His efforts laid groundwork for the enduring Catholic identity of the region and facilitated the transition of the archiepiscopal see to Lviv.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-18T11:25:38Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44292050383", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Jakub Strzemię was a Polish Roman Catholic archbishop and a professed member of the Order of Friars Minor. He served as the Archbishop of Halicz from 1392 until his death when the archdiocese was incorporated into that of the Archdiocese of Lviv.", "newValue": "Jakub Strzemię (born c. 1340 – died October 29, 1409) was a pivotal figure in the consolidation of the Latin Rite Catholic Church in Red Ruthenia, serving as the Archbishop of Halicz from 1392 until his death. A professed member of the Order of Friars Minor, Strzemię's episcopate was marked by the complex geopolitical and religious landscape of Eastern Europe at the turn of the 15th century, where the expanding Polish-Lithuanian influence met the established traditions of Eastern Orthodoxy and the remnants of the Principality of Halicz-Volhynia. His efforts were crucial in establishing and maintaining the Catholic hierarchy in a region under Polish dominion but with a predominantly Ruthenian population.

Elevated to the archiepiscopal see by Pope Boniface IX, Jakub Strzemię actively worked to organize and strengthen the Catholic diocesan structure across his extensive archdiocese, which covered much of contemporary Western Ukraine. He consecrated churches, ordained clergy, and fostered the growth of Franciscan and other monastic communities, thereby cementing the presence of the Latin Church. His administrative acumen was vital in navigating the challenges posed by the multi-confessional environment and the often-fluid borders of the era, ensuring the institutional survival and expansion of Catholicism in a contested frontier zone.

Strzemię's lasting legacy is intertwined with the ecclesiastical evolution of the region. Upon his death, the archdiocese of Halicz was formally incorporated into the newly established Archdiocese of Lviv, a move that reflected the shifting centers of power and population within Red Ruthenia. His beatification in 1790 by Pope Pius VI affirmed his spiritual importance, particularly within the Franciscan order and among Polish Catholics, solidifying his image as a holy prelate who diligently served the Church in a challenging frontier. He remains venerated as a patron saint of the Archdiocese of Lviv, a testament to his enduring influence."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-18T11:25:38Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44292050383", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1340, Kraków"}, {"title": "Spiritual seeking and religious community formation", "type": "Idea", "year": "1340, Kraków"}], "newValue": ["The expansion of Polish political and ecclesiastical influence eastward into Red Ruthenia necessitated the establishment of a robust Latin Rite hierarchy.", "The formal establishment of the Latin Archdiocese of Halicz in 1375 created a new administrative structure requiring dedicated leadership.", "The presence of the Franciscan Order in Eastern Europe provided a pool of dedicated and learned individuals suitable for high ecclesiastical office."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-18T11:25:38Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44292050383", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1409-10-29"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1409-10-29"}], "newValue": ["Jakub Strzemię's episcopate significantly strengthened the institutional presen